module test.claim.emitter_freemonoid_arm_chain_witness_test

// PERMANENT REGRESSION CONTROL for the FreeMonoid match lowering in v1.compiler.emit_rust
// (emit_native_freemonoid_match / emit_native_freemonoid_tco_match and the fm_* analysis).
//
// THE DEFECT. The lowering asked which arm mentions `Empty` and which mentions `Cons`, kept the
// FIRST answer to each, and emitted `if __fm.is_empty() { A } else { B }`. Its admission predicate
// established only that the SCRUTINEE resolves to FreeMonoid, never that the ARMS fit a two-way
// split. So a refutable field sub-pattern degraded to a wildcard, every arm after the first `Cons`
// arm was discarded, and an arm guard was dropped outright. v2.std.algebra `list_init` emitted as
// `if __fm.is_empty() { vec![] } else { vec![] }` -- one function, every input answered `[]`.
//
// WHY THIS ROW EXISTS AT ALL, because the repair's own PR argued it could not. The PR body and an
// earlier revision of the failure-mode row claimed the discriminating evidence was unavailable to
// the enrolled floor "because every claim it runs is interpreted". That is true of the defect's
// CONSEQUENCE -- a miscompiled downstream binary answering wrongly at runtime -- and FALSE of the
// repair's own subject. `emit_native_freemonoid_match` is a pure String fold, so its output is
// decidable by an interpreted claim over a fixture handed to the compiler, which is exactly what
// DESIGN section 4b directs: a state unrepresentable in the accepted corpus may still be
// representable as SOURCE HANDED TO THE COMPILER BY A FIXTURE, and if the refusal is authorable
// there, the evidence is enrollable there and declining it is specification-without-execution.
// Reported by review 69861 on gunbc#12026. The mistake was a SUBJECT CONFLATION: the harder
// question (does the emitted binary compute the right list) was used to excuse not asking the
// easy one (does the emitter emit the right arms), and that parked an available climb behind a
// capability that does not need to exist.
//
// WHAT THESE ROWS DO NOT ESTABLISH. They assert the EMITTED TEXT, not the runtime answer. The
// end-to-end behavioural evidence -- the emitted binary returning [1,2] for list_init([1,2,3]) --
// was measured on gunbc#12026 against the real emitted crate and is NOT reproduced here, because
// no enrolled claim executes emitted code. That remains the differential oracle's subject.

// THE DISCRIMINATING RED. Three arms where the middle one is a NESTED constructor in a field
// position. Pre-repair this emitted a two-way split: no length test, and the third arm's body
// absent entirely. `__fm.len() == 1` is the narrowest spelling only the repaired chain produces.
//
// THE OTHER TWO INCLUDES PIN THE THIRD ARM, AND THE FIRST ATTEMPT AT THAT WAS A DECORATION. This
// row originally asserted `drop_last(` for that job. It CANNOT GO RED: `drop_last` is the
// fixture's own function, so the emitted module carries `pub fn drop_last(` however the match
// lowers, and compile_dag_rust_emit_check is a substring read over emitted text with no rustc
// step. Worse, the sentence here named `list_tail` as the pin -- a symbol that appears nowhere in
// this file -- so the row's stated coverage argument cited a wall that was never built while the
// assertion standing in its place carried no information. DESIGN section 4b names exactly this:
// permanently green by construction, and worse than absent because it will be cited as coverage.
// It was, here and in the PR body. Found by review 70019.
//
// The gap it left is the one that matters: a lowering that kept arms one and two and dropped arm
// THREE would emit `__fm.len() == 1`, carry `pub fn drop_last(`, and pass -- which is the original
// defect in narrower form, and the exact shape this row exists to catch.
//
// The replacements are READ OFF THE EMITTED ARTIFACT rather than predicted, which is the step whose
// absence produced the decoration. Arm three is the only arm here that binds anything, so
// `Rc::new(__fm.skip(1))` is its tail binding and `drop_last(t.clone())` is its body -- the
// recursive call ON that binding. Neither is producible by the definition line, which emits
// `pub fn drop_last(xs: Rc<Vec<i64>>)`.
//
// THE TWO PINS ARE NOT EQUALLY STRONG AND THE WEAKER ONE IS NAMED AS SUCH. `Rc::new(__fm.skip(1))`
// is the GENERIC tail-binding spelling, and the pre-repair emitter emitted it too -- 10 sites in
// v2_std_algebra alone -- so it discriminates for THIS fixture rather than in general: the first
// Cons arm's tail is a nested `Empty` pattern, which the old binding reader degraded to `_`, so
// that lowering bound nothing and emitted no skip. `drop_last(t.clone())` is the unambiguous one,
// because only arm three's body carries a recursive call on a bound tail.
//
// RED-ABILITY IS MEASURED, NOT ARGUED. v2.std.algebra `list_init` carries this fixture's EXACT arm
// shape, and its retained pre-repair emission is
// `{ let __fm = xs.clone(); if __fm.is_empty() { Rc::new(vec![]) } else { Rc::new(vec![]) } }`
// with `skip(1)` appearing 0 times and `list_init(t` appearing 0 times. So both pins are absent
// from the broken lowering of this shape, and both are present in the repaired emission of the
// fixture itself. That is the check the original include never received.
test fn nested_field_arm_and_its_successor_both_reach_the_emitted_chain() -> Bool {
  compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    "module fmchain\n\nimport std.types { List, Int }\n\nfn drop_last(xs: List<Int>) -> List<Int> {\n  match xs {\n    Empty => Empty\n    Cons { head: _, tail: Empty } => Empty\n    Cons { head: h, tail: t } => Cons { head: h, tail: drop_last(xs: t) }\n  }\n}\n",
    "src/fmchain.rs",
    ["__fm.len() == 1", "Rc::new(__fm.skip(1))", "drop_last(t.clone())"],
    []
  )
}

// POSITIVE CONTROL. The ordinary two-arm shape must still lower to the native split and must NOT
// refuse. Without this row the refusal arm below is satisfiable by refusing everything, which
// would pass every must-contain-compile_error assertion while breaking all real lowering.
test fn the_ordinary_two_arm_shape_still_lowers_natively() -> Bool {
  compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    "module fmplain\n\nimport std.types { List, Int }\n\nfn head_or_zero(xs: List<Int>) -> Int {\n  match xs {\n    Empty => 0\n    Cons { head: h, tail: _ } => h\n  }\n}\n",
    "src/fmplain.rs",
    ["__fm.is_empty()"],
    ["compile_error!"]
  )
}

// REFUSAL CONTROL ONE: a refutable HEAD sub-pattern asks a question about the ELEMENT, which this
// lowering does not answer. Pre-repair it silently degraded to a wildcard and matched every
// non-empty list. It must now refuse, located, rather than mis-lower.
test fn a_refutable_head_sub_pattern_refuses_rather_than_becoming_a_wildcard() -> Bool {
  compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    "module fmhead\n\nimport std.types { List, Int }\n\ntype Box\n  = Zero\n  | One { v: Int }\n\nfn first_one(xs: List<Box>) -> Int {\n  match xs {\n    Empty => 0\n    Cons { head: One { v: n }, tail: _ } => n\n    Cons { head: _, tail: _ } => 0\n  }\n}\n",
    "src/fmhead.rs",
    ["compile_error!", "will not lower"],
    ["__fm.is_empty()"]
  )
}

// REFUSAL CONTROL TWO: an arm GUARD was dropped outright by the old lowering -- no branch body ever
// read arm_guard -- so a guarded arm silently ran unguarded. This is the third consequence of the
// class, and it appeared in neither of the two reports that found the other two.
test fn a_guarded_arm_refuses_rather_than_running_unguarded() -> Bool {
  compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    "module fmguard\n\nimport std.types { List, Int }\n\nfn big_head(xs: List<Int>) -> Int {\n  match xs {\n    Empty => 0\n    Cons { head: h, tail: _ } if h > 10 => h\n    Cons { head: _, tail: _ } => 0\n  }\n}\n",
    "src/fmguard.rs",
    ["compile_error!", "will not lower"],
    ["__fm.is_empty()"]
  )
}

// THE SECOND SPECIMEN FROM THE ASSIGNMENT, ENROLLED RATHER THAN READ ONCE. v2.lens.cost
// copied_port_derivation carries `Empty => Absent | Cons { head: only, tail: Empty } => Present |
// _ => Absent`, and pre-repair it emitted `if is_empty { None } else { Some(first) }` -- which
// answers Present for a list of ANY non-zero length, so it reported `exactly one` for a
// three-element list. That is the same class as list_init with a different consequence: one
// silently truncates a list, the other silently widens a cardinality claim, which is what makes
// this a defect in the RULE rather than at either site.
//
// THE DISCRIMINATION IS THE CHAIN, so the three-way answer is readable in the emitted text: the
// arm conditions are exactly is_empty, len() == 1, and else, so length 0 answers Absent, length 1
// answers Present, and length 3 falls past both and answers Absent. A two-way split cannot express
// that, which is why the pre-repair emission cannot distinguish 1 from 3 at all.
//
// INHABITANCE, so this fixture is not the only thing standing: the REAL site is in the emitted
// 00_compile closure as v2_lens_cost_copied_port_derivation
// derive_copied_port_index_from_arrow, and its repaired emission is
// `if __fm.is_empty() { None } else if __fm.len() == 1 { let only = (*__fm)[0].clone();
// Some(only.clone()) } else { None }` against a retained pre-repair emission of
// `if __fm.is_empty() { None } else { let only = (*__fm)[0].clone(); Some(only.clone()) }`.
// The fixture supplies the shape; the real site attests the producer emits it.
test fn the_one_element_arm_answers_only_for_length_one() -> Bool {
  compile_dag_rust_emit_check(
    "module fmcard\n\nimport std.types { List, Int }\n\nfn exactly_one(xs: List<Int>) -> Int? {\n  match xs {\n    Empty => none\n    Cons { head: only, tail: Empty } => Present { value: only }\n    _ => none\n  }\n}\n",
    "src/fmcard.rs",
    ["__fm.len() == 1", "(*__fm)[0].clone()"],
    []
  )
}

